window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"21-08","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"21-08-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-01/","title_he":"א – מצוות כתיבת ספר תורה","title_en":"1. The Mitzva of Writing a Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>Every man of Israel is commanded to write a Torah scroll for himself, as it is said (Devarim 31:19): \"Now therefore write this song for yourselves, and teach it to the children of Israel; put it in their mouths, that this song may be a witness for Me against the children of Israel\".</p>\n<p>Many explain that the song we were commanded to write is 'the Song of Ha'azinu', for it is said further on (there 31:30): \"And Moshe spoke in the ears of all the congregation of Israel the words of this song, until they were ended\", and immediately afterwards the Song of Ha'azinu is stated (Devarim 32:1-43). But since it is forbidden to write sections of the Torah (below halakha 9), in order to write 'the Song of Ha'azinu' one must write the whole Torah. Seemingly it would have been better for the Torah to command explicitly to write the whole Torah; why did it command to write 'the Song of Ha'azinu' so that we would understand from this that it is a mitzva to write the whole Torah? Rather, the Torah wished to teach us that the foundation of the whole Torah and its purpose is in 'the Song of Ha'azinu', in which we learn that the history of Israel is the history of the revelation of the word of God in the world: when Israel walk in the ways of the Torah and the mitzvot, the Divine blessing flows to them and to the whole world, and when Israel sin they are punished and go into exile from the Land. But the covenant between God and Israel is eternal, and therefore in the end God will redeem His people Israel; and the more we walk in the ways of the Torah, the more the redemption will come quickly and gently, and the more we turn our backs on the Torah, the more the redemption will be delayed and come with great suffering (Siḥot Ha-Ratziya, Devarim, p. 505). In other words, 'the Song of Ha'azinu' expresses the covenant between God and Israel, and on the basis of this covenant God gave His Torah to His people Israel, to guide them on the right path to the revelation of the word of God and His blessing to the world. And so we say in the blessings of the Torah: \"who chose us from all the peoples\" and therefore \"gave us His Torah\".</p>\n<p>In addition, the whole Torah too is called a song, because beyond the things written in it plainly, countless deep ideas are hidden in it, all of them connected with the great destiny of Israel to bring blessing to the whole world. So one can explain that the mitzva to write the song refers to the whole Torah, which is called a song, and in this respect it is an expansion of 'the Song of Ha'azinu'.<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>Even one who inherited a Torah scroll from his forefathers is commanded to write the Torah himself (Sanhedrin 21b). The Sages said (Menaḥot 30a) that whoever writes a Torah scroll, \"Scripture regards him as though he received it from Mount Sinai\". For by writing it for himself, he will merit to connect personally to the Torah that all Israel received at Sinai. And through constantly meditating on the Torah scroll he wrote, he will be strengthened in fulfilling the mitzvot written in it, and will walk in the ways of God, as it is said (Devarim 30:10): \"If you will listen to the voice of the Lord your God, to keep His commandments and His statutes <i>which are written in this book of the Torah</i>, if you will return to the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul\".</p>\n<p>The king of Israel has a special mitzva to write himself a Torah scroll in addition to the Torah scroll that every person is commanded to write, and this scroll must accompany him all his days, as it is said (Devarim 17:18-19): \"And it shall be, when he sits upon the throne of his kingdom, that he shall write himself a copy of this Torah in a book, out of that which is before the priests the Levites. And it shall be with him, and he shall read in it all the days of his life, that he may learn to fear the Lord his God, to keep all the words of this Torah and these statutes, to do them\".<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Laws of Torah Scroll 7:1) that the source of the mitzva is what is said (Devarim 31:19): \"Now therefore write this song for yourselves\", \"that is, write the Torah that contains this song, since the Torah is not written passage by passage\". And so wrote many, among them: the Semag, the Rosh, the Me'iri, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam; the Beit Yosef, the Baḥ and the Levush (Yoreh De'ah 270:1).<br>However, the whole Torah too is called a song (see Nedarim 38a). And therefore the mitzva 'write this song' can be explained as referring to the whole Torah scroll (Penei Me'irot 2, end of 179; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 270:1). They further explained that the Torah stresses \"that this song may be a witness for Me against the children of Israel\", and if it refers to the Song of Ha'azinu alone, there is no testimony in it (the Ran and Tosafot, Nedarim there). And so wrote the Radbaz (Metzudat David 82), that the whole Torah is called a 'song', \"for the Torah scroll is called the Book of the Upright (yashar), and upright (yashar) and song (shira) – it is all one thing\". Thus far. Further, that 'shira' is related to \"uprightness and evenness\", in that all the parts of the Torah fit one another in harmony, like a song (Ha-Ketav Ve-Ha-Kabbala, Devarim 31:19). Further, that a song has depth, and in order to understand it one must be helped by derash, remez and sod (the Netziv in the introduction to Ha'amek Davar, 'Kidmat Ha-Emek' 3). And so it is explained in Siḥot Ha-Ratziya, that the whole Torah is called a song, for it expresses the totality of the Torah's supremacy (Talmud Torah, p. 294)."},{"n":2,"en":"In Sanhedrin (21b) we learned that a king does not fulfil his obligation with the Torah scroll of his forefathers, and from the Gemara's answer there it is implied that this is the law for every person too. And so wrote the Rambam (Torah Scroll 7:1), the Eshkol, the Yad Rama and the Me'iri. The Baḥ (270:1) explained that the law is learned from the word 'for yourselves' (lakhem) in the plural, \"that each and every man of Israel is obligated to write a Torah scroll for himself\". In Sefer Ha-Ḥinukh (613) he explained the reason: \"So that the scrolls may multiply among us, and we may lend some of them to one who cannot afford to buy, and also so that each and every one of Israel may read in new scrolls, lest they grow weary of reading in the old scrolls that their fathers left them.\" However, the Rif, the Rosh and the Tur omitted this law, and the Maharshag (2:205) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (270:10) explained that in their view a person fulfils the obligation even with a scroll he inherited from his forefathers, just as one fulfils it with a scroll bought from the market (below note 4). Following this, the Yad Sha'ul (270, s.v. 'Afilu hiniḥu lo') explained that today, even for those who hold like the Rambam that the mitzva has not been uprooted (as explained in the next halakha), if he inherited a Torah scroll from his forefathers he need not write one."}]},{"slug":"21-08-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-02/","title_he":"ב – המצווה בזמן הזה","title_en":"2. The Mitzva in Our Time","body_en":"<p>At first it was permitted to write only the Written Torah, that is, the twenty-four books of the Tanakh (Torah, Prophets and Writings). And God commanded that each one of Israel write the Torah for himself in ink on parchment according to the laws of writing STaM, and the learned also wrote the Nakh (Prophets and Writings), and in these books they studied all the days of their lives. And it was forbidden to write the Oral Torah, so that it would be alive in people's hearts and preserved in memory. But after the generations declined and the words of the Oral Torah expanded and branched out into a multitude of views, and the people began to be scattered into the various exiles, the Sages of Israel saw that in order to sustain the Torah in Israel it was necessary to permit the writing of the Oral Torah (below halakha 8). So the Mishna was written, and after it the Jerusalem and Babylonian Talmuds, the midrashim, the commentaries and the halakhic rulings, until the main study is carried out in the books of the Oral Torah. Since they permitted writing the Oral Torah, they permitted writing the Tanakh too in ordinary script and not on parchment. Nearly six hundred years ago printing was introduced to the world, and by a gradual process the price of books became cheaper; as a result, more people could buy books, and everyone studies in them.</p>\n<p>Following the fact that Torah study is carried out in the books of the Oral Torah, already in the days of the Rishonim the question arose: how is it right to fulfil the mitzva?</p>\n<p>Some say that even after people began to study in other books the mitzva remained in place (Rambam, Laws of Torah Scroll 7:1). That is, the main aim of the mitzva is to write the sanctified Torah scroll, so that by writing it every Jew will connect to the Torah as it was transmitted at Sinai, and as the Sages said (Menaḥot 30a), that whoever writes a Torah scroll, \"Scripture regards him as though he received it from Mount Sinai\" (Pele Yo'etz, 'Sefer').</p>\n<p>On the other hand, some say that the mitzva was intended so that every person of Israel would be able to study the Torah and fulfil its mitzvot, as it is said (Devarim 31:19): \"Now therefore write this song for yourselves, and teach it to the children of Israel; put it in their mouths\". When all study was carried out in the sanctified Torah scroll, by nature the scroll would wear out within one to three generations. And since the Torah commanded everyone able to do so to write a Torah scroll for himself, many of Israel had the possibility of studying Torah. One who was able to write merited to study in the scroll he wrote for himself, and others studied in the scrolls left from previous generations. But from the time it was permitted to write the Oral Torah, the mitzva is fulfilled by buying the books in which the Torah is actually studied, and there is no mitzva for every person to write a Torah scroll (the Rosh, Torah Scroll 1; Derisha 270:4; Shakh 5). However, every community must see to it that it has a Torah scroll, in order to read in it the readings that the Sages instituted (Nefesh Ḥaya, Yoreh De'ah 75).</p>\n<p>In halakha it was ruled according to both views (Shulḥan Arukh, Yoreh De'ah 270:1-2), so that in practice the mitzva has two parts. The first: it is a mitzva to write a Torah scroll, and today the mitzva is fulfilled mainly by partnership in financing the writing of the sanctified Torah scroll in ink on parchment, as will be explained in the next halakha. The second: every Jew is commanded to buy for himself the basic Torah books so that he can study in them, as will be explained below (in halakha 4).<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Some wrote that the mitzva is to write a Torah scroll in ink on parchment; so wrote the Rambam (Torah Scroll 7:1), the Semag (positive commandments 24) and the Semak (155). For thereby he will connect to the Torah as it was given at Sinai. Further, that by human nature, after he has laboured in writing the scroll – he will love to study in it (Sefer Ha-Batim, Migdal David, positive commandments 16).<br>However, in the view of the Rosh (Torah Scroll 1), since in our days the Torah scroll is kept in the synagogue for the public Torah reading, the Torah mitzva is that every man of Israel should have books in which he actually studies – \"the Ḥumashim of the Torah, the Mishna and the Gemara and their commentaries, to meditate in them, he and his sons. For the mitzva of writing the Torah is to study in it, as it is written: and teach it to the children of Israel; put it in their mouths. And through the Gemara and the commentaries he will know the explanation of the mitzvot and the laws thoroughly; therefore these are the books that a person is commanded to write, and also not to sell them except in order to study Torah and to marry a wife\". And so wrote Rabbeinu Yeruḥam (Netiv 2, part 2, 17:2), and he wrote that so the Geonim wrote. And so wrote the Tur (siman 270). (On the law of selling printed books in our days see below 9:8, note 11.)<br>The Beit Yosef (270:2) wondered about the Rosh: how could he exempt one from the mitzva in its plain sense? And he explained that apparently the Rosh holds that in addition to the mitzva incumbent on every person to write a Torah scroll, it is a mitzva that he buy the books in which he will actually study. And so wrote the Levush 2; the Taz 4; the Divrei Ḥamudot 5; the Yad Ha-Ketana (Laws of Torah Scroll 4, Minḥat Ani 5); the Beit Yehuda (Yoreh De'ah 23); the Gra (270:4); and the Ru'aḥ Ḥayim of Palache 3. And so wrote the Shulḥan Gavoha 12, and he added that according to those who hold that there is a mitzva to write a Torah scroll, there is a virtue in a person studying from the sanctified Torah scroll.<br>However, simply, according to the Rosh and the Tur, since the mitzva is part of the mitzva of Torah study, when in practice people study in printed books, there is no obligation for every person to write a Torah scroll. And so wrote the Derisha 4, and he added in the Perisha 8 that in the past they were precise in understanding the Oral Torah from the Written Torah, but today, when we study in other books, one should not demean the sanctified Torah scroll by studying in it needlessly, and consequently the mitzva for every person to write himself a Torah scroll has lapsed. And so wrote the Shakh (270:5), the Penei Me'irot (3:20), the Nefesh Ḥaya (Yoreh De'ah 75) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 9.<br>In halakha, in the Shulḥan Arukh 270:1 he brought the words of the Rambam, and in se'if 2 the words of the Rosh. And so emerges from most of the Aḥaronim, that it is a mitzva to fulfil the words of both. However, some of them wrote that the main mitzva is to write a Torah scroll in ink on parchment, and a branch extending from it is to acquire sacred books for the sake of study. So wrote the Birkei Yosef (270:12) and the Yad Ha-Ketana (Laws of Torah Scroll 4, Minḥat Ani 5). And similarly in the Pele Yo'etz (entry 'Sefer'). And some of them wrote that the main mitzva is that a person have printed books in which he will study, and in addition there is also a mitzva to write a Torah scroll in ink on parchment. So wrote the Benei Yona (270:6-7), and he proved this from the fact that it is permitted to sell a Torah scroll for the sake of Torah study, so study is the main thing. And so wrote the Yad Sha'ul (270, s.v. 'Ve-ha'idna mitzva likhtov'), the Ḥayei Adam (31:49-50) and the Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh (28:1-2)."}]},{"slug":"21-08-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-03/","title_he":"ג – כתיבת ספר התורה המקודש בשותפות","title_en":"3. Writing the Sanctified Torah Scroll in Partnership","body_en":"<p>One who hires a scribe to write a Torah scroll for him is considered as though he wrote it. And even if the scribe has almost finished writing the scroll, as long as there remains one letter in it that needs to be corrected, he can still buy the scroll from him and hire him to complete its writing as his agent, and thereby fulfil the mitzva properly. But if he buys a Torah scroll whose writing has already been completed, although after the fact he has fulfilled the mitzva, he has not fulfilled it properly. For the scroll was completed not as his agent, that is, without his meriting to be a partner in its writing, and the Sages said that he is \"like one who snatches a mitzva from the market\" (Menaḥot 30a, Rashi and Tosafot).<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>In the past, when the main study was done in a Torah scroll written on parchment in the manner of its transmission from Sinai, one may suppose that many learned to write STaM script and wrote a Torah scroll for themselves. And some preferred to hire a scribe to write a Torah scroll for them. And although it involved a great investment of time or money, since the main study was done in the Torah scroll, they were ready to invest the time or money required to write it. But today, when people are not accustomed to study from the sanctified Torah scroll, some say that it is right to fulfil the mitzva in partnership, in such a way that many people participate in financing the writing of the Torah scroll. After its writing is completed they will lend it to the synagogue for the Torah reading, and so they will continue to fulfil the mitzva with it all the days of their lives.</p>\n<p>And although some say that even today, when people study in printed books, everyone is commanded to write a Torah scroll for himself, in practice one may rely on those who hold that the mitzva can be fulfilled in partnership. And this is preferable, for otherwise holy scrolls will multiply for which there will be no need for the Torah reading, and there is a concern that they will be treated with contempt for lack of a dignified place to keep them. Therefore, only a wealthy person who can easily finance the writing of a Torah scroll, and knows that there is a synagogue that lacks a Torah scroll, should finance the writing of a Torah scroll for it. Likewise, a wealthy person who wants to have a Torah scroll in his house so that he can honour it and read in it occasionally – it is a mitzva for him to hire a scribe to write him a Torah scroll.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Menaḥot (30a): \"Rabbi Yehoshua bar Abba said in the name of Rav Giddel in the name of Rav: One who buys a Torah scroll from the market – is like one who snatches a mitzva from the market; if he wrote it – Scripture regards him as though he received it from Mount Sinai. Rav Sheshet said: If he corrected even one letter, it is regarded as though he wrote it.\" Since without this the scroll would have been invalid (Tosafot). The Nimukei Yosef explained that the reason one should not buy a ready-made Torah scroll is that he does not exert himself for the mitzva, \"and there is room for the attribute of judgement to object and say that had it not come to him without effort – he would not have done it\". But if \"he wrote it himself, or hired a scribe to write it and exerted himself in preparing the parchments and in bearing the burden of the scribe until it was written\", it is considered as though he received it from Mount Sinai, for he exerted himself in something like the exertion of \"going through the wilderness in order to receive the Torah from Mount Sinai\". In the Seridei Esh (3, 97:2) he explained, based on the Ḥinukh (613), that the mitzva is that Torah scrolls should multiply, and one who buys from the market does not act so that Torah scrolls will multiply.<br>In the view of many, although one who buys a ready-made Torah scroll is considered one who snatches a mitzva, he has fulfilled the mitzva. So wrote Rashi, the Me'iri and the Nimukei Yosef. And so emerges from Tosafot, the Rosh, the Semag, the Mordekhai and the Hagahot Maimoniyot. And so wrote the Baḥ (270:2), the Minḥat Ḥinukh (613:2) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (270:2). And some say that one who buys a ready-made Torah scroll has not fulfilled the mitzva, because the Torah scroll must be his at the time of writing. So wrote the Eshkol in the name of Rabbi Yitzḥak ibn Ghiyat, and so it is implied in the Yad Rama. And so wrote the Rema (270:1). And so wrote many regarding the Rambam's view (Beit Yehuda 1:23; Minḥat Ḥinukh there; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 3).<br>Some say that one who hires a scribe to write a Torah scroll for him is considered as one who wrote it himself (the Nimukei Yosef; the Me'iri, Kiryat Sefer, Ma'amar 4, part 1; Rema 270:1; the Levush). And some say that there is a greater virtue for one who writes it himself, and if he does not know how – he should hire a scribe (Rambam, Laws of Torah Scroll 7:1; the Ḥinukh 613)."},{"n":5,"en":"Some of the Aḥaronim wondered why many, including God-fearing rabbis, do not fulfil the mitzva of writing a Torah scroll (see Be'er Sheva, Be'er Mayim Ḥayim 16; Hit'orerut Teshuva 4:147). Apparently, they were accustomed to rely on the Rosh as explained by the Derisha and those with him (above note 3). In addition, many were accustomed to fulfil the mitzva of writing a Torah scroll in partnership. And indeed many hold that it is not possible to fulfil the mitzva in partnership (Beit Yehuda, Yoreh De'ah 23; Mar'eh Ha-Nogah of Rabbi Refael Ashkenazi, 6b-c; Pele Yo'etz, 'Sefer'; Tuv Ta'am Va-Da'at, first edition 232; Ru'aḥ Ḥayim of Palache 270:6; Responsa Mahari Ashkenazi, Oraḥ Ḥayim 7; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 11). But on the other hand, many hold that the mitzva can be fulfilled in partnership (Pardes David of Rabbi David Dishbek, parashat Ki Tetze, brought in the Pitḥei Teshuva 270:1; Da'at Kedoshim 270:1; Sho'el U-Meshiv, first edition 1:266; Nefesh Ḥaya, Yoreh De'ah 75; Ḥamesh Yadot of Rabbi Binyamin Ledzman 14:1:7; Igrot Moshe 1, Yoreh De'ah 163). In practice, since a Torah scroll is very expensive, it is right to rely on those who hold that the mitzva can be fulfilled in partnership. For in addition to the fact that according to the Rosh, as explained by the Derisha and those with him, there is today no mitzva to write a Torah scroll in ink on parchment, as explained in the previous halakha, the Rishonim wrote that this mitzva is incumbent \"on a man of Israel who can afford it\" (the Rosh, Torah Scroll 1; and see Igrot Moshe, Yoreh De'ah 1:163). Therefore, one who is not very wealthy should rightly fulfil the mitzva in partnership.<br>Some say that even if the owners of the scroll gave it as a gift to a synagogue, they have fulfilled the mitzva by financing its writing (Benei Yona 270:4; Sho'el U-Meshiv, first edition 1:266; Nefesh Ḥaya, Yoreh De'ah 75). But many hold that if the scroll passes into the ownership of the synagogue, the mitzva has lapsed for them (the Maharal of Prague in his glosses on the Tur; Torat Ḥayim, Sanhedrin 21b; Yad Efrayim, glosses on Oraḥ Ḥayim 153:10; Ru'aḥ Ḥayim of Palache 270:8; Avnei Nezer, Oraḥ Ḥayim 40:6; Nahar Mitzrayim, Torah Scroll 8; Neveh Shalom, Torah Scroll 3, and others). Therefore, although it is fitting to dedicate the scroll to the use of the synagogue as the gabba'im decide, it is right that they stipulate that the scroll remains the property of its owners for ever; and so wrote Yabi'a Omer (7, Yoreh De'ah 25).<br>I wrote that a wealthy person who can easily finance the writing of a Torah scroll on his own should do so only when he knows that there is a synagogue that needs one, or he himself wants to honour the scroll in his house. But if he does not want this, it is better that he not finance a Torah scroll, both because there is a concern that the scroll will not be honoured properly, and because it is better that he donate for the support of Torah study and the publication of books in which people will study. As the Ḥayei Adam wrote (31:50), that in our time the mitzva to support Torah scholars is preferable to the mitzva of writing a Torah scroll. And so wrote Responsa Sho'el Ve-Nish'al (4, Yoreh De'ah 66), and he proved it from the fact that when necessary it is permitted to sell a Torah scroll in order to finance Torah study (below 9:8), all the more so that from the outset it is better to donate to the support of Torah scholars. And in the Ru'aḥ Ḥayim (270:3) he wrote that one can fulfil the mitzva by participating in printing a book of the teaching of the Rishonim or the Aḥaronim, in which many will study, in accordance with the purpose of the mitzva. And this is included in what they said (Ketubot 50a) on the verse (Tehillim 112:3): \"and his righteousness endures for ever\" – \"this is one who writes the Torah, the Prophets and the Writings and lends them to others\". This principle is true also for family members who want to commemorate their relatives, when there is no synagogue that needs a new Torah scroll (see Yabi'a Omer 8, Yoreh De'ah 36)."}]},{"slug":"21-08-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-04/","title_he":"ד – המצווה שתהיה ספריית קודש בכל בית","title_en":"4. The Mitzva to Have a Library of Sacred Books in Every Home","body_en":"<p>As we have learned, the second part of the mitzva of writing a Torah scroll is fulfilled by every Jew buying himself sacred books in order to study the Torah in them, and through this to walk in the ways of God and fulfil His mitzvot, and to add goodness and blessing to the world. Therefore, every Jew is obligated to devote a place in his home to a library of sacred books, and to fill it with books through which he can study the Torah that God gave to Israel. The question is which books every person must have in addition to the Tanakh, for there are many books, from the words of the Sages in the Mishna, the Gemara and the halakhic and aggadic midrashim, to the books of the Rishonim and the Aḥaronim in the interpretation of the Tanakh, the Gemara, halakhic rulings, and also books of faith and ethics.</p>\n<p>In principle, every person must keep the best books for him for fulfilling the mitzva of Torah study, which obligates every Jew to know the foundations of faith and of the mitzvot down to the practical halakha. Included in the foundations of faith is the study of the Tanakh, with its story of the Divine revelation to the people of Israel about the value of man and his destiny, the creation of the people of Israel and its destiny to settle the Land of Israel, to walk in the way of God and to bring blessing to all the families of the earth. The study of faith also includes the study of ethics and good character traits. Likewise, one must study all the mitzvot of the Torah and their meaning, with the fences and enactments that the Sages established for them, down to the practical halakha; while the practical halakhot that a person encounters frequently require deeper and more detailed study.</p>\n<p>As the years went by and books multiplied, the question became more complex. And it appears in practice that it is not possible to determine which books every Jew is obligated to buy, for the Sages said (Avoda Zara 19a): \"A person learns Torah only from the place his heart desires\", that is, in the books suited to him. Therefore the Sages of Israel in every generation took the trouble to write books of different kinds on faith and halakha, in order to give a precise answer for understanding a particular area of the Torah and sometimes even for understanding it as a whole. And on every Jew rests the responsibility to buy himself the books through which he can study all the foundations of the Torah, in faith and in halakha, and in which he can go back and meditate so as not to forget the Torah and so as to continue to deepen in it. And one who has difficulty choosing the books suited to him would do well to consult about it with a rav who knows him. Heads of families must see to it that there are in their home books suited to all the members of the family, each according to his level and area of interest.</p>\n<p>In addition, it is good to buy the basic works, such as the Mishna, the Talmud, the Rambam and the Shulḥan Arukh with commentaries, and likewise the basic works of faith. And although there is no obligation to buy them when he does not intend to study them in the foreseeable future, ideally it is good that they be in his home, in the hope that he will occasionally look up a particular source in them, and perhaps even merit to study them.</p>\n<p>It appears that the mitzva cannot be fulfilled with computerised databases, because on Shabbatot and festivals, which are intended especially for Torah study, people study in books. Moreover, the books a person buys are dear to him, and with their help he can study with greater desire and concentration. In addition, when the books stand in a dignified place in the home, they remind all the members of the family of the Torah, which sets a vision before them and lights their way, and they rouse the members of the household to study in them. Therefore, the obligation of the mitzva is that every person have a library of sacred books through which he can study the Torah. And it is a mitzva that he buy books that are beautiful and pleasant for study, and place them in a beautiful bookcase, as it is said (Shemot 15:2): \"This is my God and I will glorify Him\" – \"Beautify yourself before Him in mitzvot\" (Shabbat 133b).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"21-08-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-05/","title_he":"ה – המצווה לנשים ולבני הבית","title_en":"5. The Mitzva for Women and for the Members of the Household","body_en":"<p>As mentioned, the mitzva has two parts – the first, participating in financing the writing of a Torah scroll. From this part women are exempt; however, if they participate in financing its writing – they fulfil a mitzva. The second part of the mitzva is buying books for the sake of Torah study, and in this part it is reasonable that women too are obligated. In the past, indeed, most women did not learn to read, and relied on the men, who bore the responsibility for Torah study and for teaching halakha in the home. But in recent centuries, by a gradual process that heralds redemption, women began to bear a growing responsibility in the home and in society. This included learning to read, and later also learning all the professions. And since the obligation to keep the Torah and its mitzvot applies to women as to men, it is clear as a matter of course that they are obligated to study Torah so that they can keep the Torah and carry forward the word of God and His blessing in all areas of life. Therefore, women are obligated to study Torah as needed for conducting life according to the Torah. For this purpose, every woman must have a library of sacred books, containing all the books she needs for guidance in life in halakha, Tanakh, faith and ethics.</p>\n<p>A married couple fulfils the mitzva with their shared books. Their sons and daughters who have reached the age of mitzvot too, as long as they live in their parents' home, fulfil their obligation with their books, and when they marry and set up their home they too will become obligated to set up a library of sacred books in their home. Single people who live together in an apartment may make do with one library.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"In Sefer Ha-Ḥinukh (mitzva 613) it is explained that the obligation of writing a Torah scroll is incumbent on males only, who are obligated in the mitzva of Torah study every day and every night, but women are exempt from writing a Torah scroll. And so wrote the Maharam Schick (on the 613 mitzvot). On the other hand, according to the Sha'agat Aryeh 35, women are obligated in the mitzva of writing a Torah scroll, for women are exempt only from theoretical study that is not needed for guidance in a life of Torah and faith, but in the study of practical halakha and the foundations of faith and ethics – they are obligated, and therefore women recite the blessings of the Torah every morning. Moreover, the mitzva of writing a Torah scroll does not depend on the mitzva of Torah study, but is a mitzva in its own right. And just as women are obligated in mezuza although they are not fit to write it, so they are obligated to have a Torah scroll. And so wrote the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan (270:6), and he inferred from the Rambam that women are obligated. And in the Minḥat Ḥinukh (613:9) he wrote that according to the Derisha (281:1; brought in the Shakh 281:6) in the view of the Rosh, women are fit to write a Torah scroll, and consequently obligated in the mitzva, but according to the other poskim women are unfit to write a Torah scroll (as explained below 10:1), and therefore exempt from the mitzva of writing it. And so wrote many that women are exempt, among them: the Shiyurei Berakha (270:1), the Da'at Kedoshim (270:2), the Keset Ha-Sofer (1, Lishkat Ha-Sofer 3) and the Teshuva Me-Ahava (1:24). And so is the view of the Yad Sha'ul (270:1), and he added that \"for the most part, what a woman acquires – her husband acquires\", and consequently she cannot be obligated. However, it is clear that a woman who donates a Torah scroll or participates in financing it fulfils a mitzva (Nahar Mitzrayim, Torah Scroll 10). All this concerns the first part of the mitzva; but regarding buying sacred books in which women actually need to study, they are obligated, as explained above (and see Da'at Kohen 168). And although there is no source for this, since with the increase of Torah and wisdom the obligation of women in the mitzva of Torah study has grown and become much stronger, and the mitzva of buying books is intended for the sake of Torah study, it follows that women must have books in which they can fulfil their mitzva of Torah study."}]},{"slug":"21-08-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-06/","title_he":"ו – מסורת כתיבת ספר התורה","title_en":"6. The Tradition of Writing the Torah Scroll","body_en":"<p>Towards his death, Moshe Rabbeinu wrote the Torah in a book, and instructed the priests, the sons of Levi, to place it beside the Ark of the Covenant in the Holy of Holies, as a testimony and to prevent forgery, as it is said (Devarim 31:24-26): \"And it came to pass, when Moshe had finished writing the words of this Torah in a book, until they were finished, that Moshe commanded the Levites who carried the Ark of the Covenant of the Lord, saying: Take this book of the Torah, and put it by the side of the Ark of the Covenant of the Lord your God, that it may be there for a witness against you.\" The Sages said (Devarim Rabba, Vayelekh 9:9) that apart from the Torah scroll that was placed beside the Ark of the Covenant, Moshe wrote another twelve Torah scrolls and handed them to the twelve tribes of Israel, and from them the members of the tribes copied the Torah for themselves.</p>\n<p>One of the wonders of the history of Israel is the precise tradition of the text of the Torah scroll. Despite the scattering of the exiles and the changes in customs and ways of life between the communities of Israel, the tradition of writing the Torah scroll remained uniform. And although Torah scrolls were copied scroll from scroll over thousands of years, in different places in the world and in the course of wandering from exile to exile, all the scrolls are the same in the order of the words and the letters. All this is because of the enormous importance that halakha attaches to precision in writing the Torah, for any Torah scroll that lacks one letter, or has one letter added – is entirely invalid. Even when the change of letter does not change the reading or the meaning of the word, for example where in the Torah the word is written defectively and the scribe wrote it in full spelling – the Torah scroll is invalid (Menaḥot 29b; below 10:2).</p>\n<p>Despite the wondrous precision in copying the Torah, which has no parallel in any nation or language, in the wake of the exiles a few differences arose between the traditions. For example: in the book of Bereshit (9:29), in the tradition of those of Sephardi and Ashkenazi origin the text is: \"<i>Va-yehi</i> kol yemei Noaḥ\", whereas in the tradition of those of Yemenite origin the text is: \"<i>Va-yihyu</i> kol yemei Noaḥ\". And likewise in the book of Devarim (23:2), in the tradition of the immigrants from Spain and most of those of Ashkenazi origin it is written: \"petzu'a dakka<i>h</i>\" with a 'heh'. And in the tradition of the immigrants from Yemen, and likewise in several old Torah scrolls from Ashkenaz, it is written: \"dakka<i>'</i>\" with an 'alef'. And likewise in the book of Vayikra, according to the tradition prevalent among most of Israel, an open passage begins in chapter 7 verse 28 (below 10:16), and according to the tradition of the immigrants from Yemen and other traditions, the passage begins earlier, at verse 22 (see Shakh 275:6).</p>\n<p>The Rambam wrote that the scroll on which he relies is the scroll known in Egypt, which was previously in Jerusalem, and from which the scrolls were corrected, and on which everyone relied, because the famous Masorete 'Ben Asher' examined it for years and corrected it many times (Laws of Torah Scroll 8:4). That is, in the Rambam's view there is one version that is the correct one, and according to it all scrolls must be written. According to the tradition of the Aleppo community, the famous scroll of which the Rambam spoke is the 'Aleppo Codex' (Keter Aram Tzova). Sadly, we have today only parts of the 'Aleppo Codex', but there are many scrolls that were copied from it, and on the basis of a comparison of all the books of the Tanakh, it has been found that the scrolls of the immigrants from Yemen are the closest to its original text.</p>\n<p>In practice, the greatest of the Rishonim wrote that one should not prefer the tradition of Ben Asher over the other traditions, and every authoritative tradition according to which many scrolls have been written over generations has its place, and one who writes according to it – his scroll is valid (the Rashba, the Me'iri, the Mahari Mintz). And likewise regarding the Torah reading, it is agreed in practice that any scroll written according to one of the authoritative traditions may be read from with a blessing by members of all the communities.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"Already in the days of the Talmud a few differences arose in the tradition of writing defective and full spellings, and nevertheless all those called up to the Torah recited the blessings of the Torah (Kiddushin 30a). According to the Rashba (in the responsa attributed to the Ramban 232), the Me'iri (Kiddushin 30a) and Responsa Mahari Mintz (Fiorda 8), every authoritative tradition is valid, and one may recite the blessing over it. And even the Rambam, who holds that the tradition of Ben Asher is the precise one, wrote in a responsum (Blau 294) that in a pressing situation it is permitted to read from an invalid Torah scroll and even to recite the blessing over it. And his son Rabbi Avraham wrote (Responsa, siman 91) that one should not invalidate for the Torah reading scrolls written according to another tradition, for we do not have the scroll that Ezra the Scribe wrote, by which we could invalidate other scrolls.<br>Regarding the tradition of writing, we rely on the scrolls that are in our hands, for the main fulfilment of the Torah is in the form in which it is found in our hands. Therefore, even if there is a midrash from which it is implied that the version differs from the version before us, the halakha follows the majority of scrolls in our days (Responsa Maharam Ḥalawa 144)."}]},{"slug":"21-08-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-07/","title_he":"ז – תורה שבכתב ואימתי מותר לאומרה על פה","title_en":"7. The Written Torah, and When It May Be Said by Heart","body_en":"<p>The Torah we received at Sinai is made up of two parts: the Written Torah, and the Oral Torah, which explains the Written Torah. Since the Written Torah was given in writing and the Oral Torah was transmitted by word of mouth, it is a mitzva to take care to study the words of the Written Torah from the written text and not to say them by heart, and to study the words of the Oral Torah by heart, and not to write them down. As it is said (Shemot 34:27): \"And the Lord said to Moshe: <i>Write</i> for yourself these words, for <i>by the mouth of</i> these words I have made a covenant with you and with Israel\". The Sages expounded (Gittin 60b): \"Words that are written you are not permitted to say by heart\", since of them it is said 'write for yourself', and \"words that are oral you are not permitted to say in writing\", since of them it is said 'by the mouth of these words'.</p>\n<p>The reason one should not say words of the Written Torah by heart is that one must be very precise in studying the Written Torah, for in every letter countless ideas and meanings are hidden, and in order not to lose them one must take care to study them from the book (the Ritva and the Ran). In addition, reading from the written text gives authority to what is written, and through this the unlearned are strengthened more in their faith in the Torah (Yere'im 268). In addition, the Torah must appear in its wholeness, and when the learner reads it from the book, he reads the verse he is studying out of its complete and perfect appearance. But when he says it by heart, at any moment he says only a few words, and does not connect to its complete appearance (the Maharal, Ḥiddushei Aggadot there).</p>\n<p>However, the verses of prayer, such as the Shema and Pesukei De-Zimra, may be said by heart. Some say this is because these verses are fluent in everyone's mouth and there is no concern that people will err in them (Rabbeinu Tam; Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 49:1), and some say that the prohibition on saying written words by heart applies only when one discharges the obligation of the public through them (Tosafot, Temura 14b, s.v. 'Devarim'; the Yere'im). Based on this view, many are accustomed to memorise various verses for themselves by heart, such as 'the Song of Ha'azinu' and 'the Song of Devora'.</p>\n<p>However, when one studies verses and derives words of halakha from their precise wording, it is forbidden to say them by heart. And the fact that many rabbis are accustomed, in the course of a sermon, to cite verses by heart is because they do not intend to examine them and derive from their precise wording, but to cite them as a support; in such a case, so as not to trouble the public to wait until the book is opened, one may be lenient and say them by heart (see Mishna Berura 49:3).<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"The Rishonim asked: how is it that we are accustomed to recite verses by heart in the course of prayer? And they answered that what is fluent in everyone's mouth may be said by heart (Or Zarua 1, 545, and the Mordekhai, in the name of Rabbeinu Tam). And many say that the prohibition applies to words with which one discharges the obligation of others (Tosafot, Temura 14b, s.v. 'Devarim'; the Yere'im, the Semag, the Rosh). Similarly it is explained in Yoma (70a) that the High Priest would read the main Torah reading of Yom Kippur from two places in the book of Vayikra, but the addition from the book of Bamidbar he said by heart, so as not to trouble the public to wait until they rolled the Torah to there. And they explain, based on the Yerushalmi (Yoma 7:1), that the verses he said from the book of Bamidbar were said in the manner of the order of prayer and not for the purpose of discharging the public's obligation of reading the Torah, and therefore in a time of need he would say them by heart (the Riva in Tosafot Yeshanim, Yoma 70a, s.v. 'U-ve-asor'; the Ritva there; the Semag)."}]},{"slug":"21-08-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-08/","title_he":"ח – תורה שבעל פה וההיתר לכותבה","title_en":"8. The Oral Torah and the Permission to Write It","body_en":"<p>As we learned in the previous halakha, by the basic law it was forbidden to write the Oral Torah, as it is said (Shemot 34:27): \"for <i>by the mouth of</i> these words I have made a covenant with you and with Israel\". The Sages expounded (Gittin 60b): \"Words that are oral you are not permitted to say in writing\". However, on a personal level, the prophets and the Sages wrote down for themselves the traditions that had been transmitted to them from the Sages of the previous generations. They also wrote down for themselves explanations that had newly occurred to them, and these writings were called 'hidden scrolls' (megillot setarim) (Rashi, Shabbat 6b). For the prohibition was to write the words of the Oral Torah as an authoritative book from which one teaches the public (Rambam in his introduction to the Mishneh Torah; the Netziv, Devarim 30:14).</p>\n<p>As for the reason for the prohibition, they explained that the aim of the Oral Torah is to be precise and to go deeper into the meaning of the Written Torah, and not that people should be precise in its wording instead of in the Written Torah. And when the Oral Torah is written down, the emphasis is liable to be shifted to precision in its wording instead of in the Written Torah (see the Ritva, Gittin 60b; the Ikkarim 3:23; the Perisha, Oraḥ Ḥayim 49:1). In addition, it is impossible for the Written Torah to address all the matters that arise anew from generation to generation, and it is necessary that the Sages of Israel go deeply into the Written Torah and into the foundations of the Oral Torah, in order to instruct each generation according to its circumstances. And if they were to write down the instruction they gave for their generation, the instruction would become fixed and would hold back the Sages of the next generation from instructing their generation as befits it (Rav Glasner in his introduction to Responsa Dor Revi'i; and see the Ikkarim 3:23). That is, the Oral Torah is meant to carry the words of the Written Torah, which are heavenly and absolute, into our lives in this world, and this is the uniqueness of Israel (Tanḥuma, Ki Tisa 34). And in order that it be preserved in its full vitality and be able to be reflected in all the human shades of our lives, it is forbidden to write it down and fix it in a defined formulation.</p>\n<p>After the destruction of the Second Temple, the Sages saw that the people of Israel was becoming more and more scattered among the nations, the wicked kingdom ruled over Israel, and the hearts of the students were diminishing, and they came to the conclusion that in order to sustain the Torah in Israel it was necessary to write down the Oral Torah. At first they made do with writing the Mishna. Afterwards they saw that there was a need to write the Talmuds and the midrashim, and later they permitted writing, without limit, all the explanations that had newly arisen within the framework of the Oral Torah in all areas of the Torah, including: responsa to questions, commentaries on the Talmud, explanations of Scripture, halakhic rulings, books of thought and ethics, Jewish philosophy and Kabbala. However, by taking care to write down the different views, they left room for the Sages of the following generations to go deeply into the different views and to instruct their generation as befits it.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"21-08-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-09/","title_he":"ט – כתיבת חומשים, פרשיות ופסוקים מהתורה","title_en":"9. Writing Single Books, Passages and Verses from the Torah","body_en":"<p>Although the Torah comprises all five books of the Torah, and if even one letter is missing from it, it is entirely invalid, in order to make study easier people were sometimes accustomed to write each book on its own; but the sanctity of these single books is not like the sanctity of a complete Torah scroll (below 9:13).</p>\n<p>But it is forbidden to write passages out of the Torah scroll, for the Torah must appear in its wholeness, as it is said (Tehillim 19:8): \"The Torah of the Lord is perfect, restoring the soul\" – when it is perfect (whole) it restores the soul, and not when it appears in a partial form. Only when one writes a complete book is there a certain complete appearance in it, but less than that it is fragmentary. In the days of the Sages, even for the purpose of teaching children they did not permit writing passages (Gittin 60a). And some ruled so in halakha (Rambam, the Rosh, Shulḥan Arukh 283:2).</p>\n<p>However, in practice, the view of most poskim is that on the basis of the permission to write the Oral Torah for the sake of study, it is also permitted to write passages from the Written Torah for the sake of students (the Rif, the Ra'avya, the Mordekhai, the Baḥ, the Taz 283:1; Shakh 3). Included in this, it was also permitted to print siddurim in which passages of Torah are brought.<sup>9</sup></p>\n<p>But for a minor purpose such as creating an educational atmosphere, some say it is forbidden to write verses, since the whole permission is only for the great need of Torah study (the Taz, the Birkei Yosef). However, in practice the custom follows those who hold that since it has been permitted to write verses, it is permitted to write them also for a minor purpose such as creating an educational atmosphere. For example, to write the verse (Vayikra 23:42): \"You shall dwell in booths seven days\", in order to hang it as a decoration in the sukka (Bikkurei Ya'akov). Or to write the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy in order to hang them on the wall of the synagogue, or to write verses on charity boxes and the like (Benei Yona, Rav Uziel). And it is right to write these verses not in STaM script, because some say that the prohibition on writing parts of the Torah applies specifically to STaM script.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In Gittin (60a), Abaye asked Rabba: \"May one write a scroll (a passage of the Torah) for a child to learn from?\" Rabba answered that one does not write it. In addition to the general explanation for this, the Ḥatam Sofer added in his Ḥiddushim on Gittin there that when the book is not complete, one loses the learning that can be derived from the juxtaposition of the passages. In practice, so ruled the Rambam (Torah Scroll 7:14), the Rosh (Gittin 5:20, according to the Tur) and the Shulḥan Arukh (Yoreh De'ah 283:2), that it is forbidden to write scrolls of the Torah even for the purpose of teaching children.<br>However, the Rif wrote that in halakha one does write a scroll to learn from. The Ran, the Me'iri and others explained that they permitted this because of the great need involved, just as they permitted writing the Oral Torah, as is explained in Gittin (60a), that Rabbi Yoḥanan and Reish Lakish \"would look into a book of aggada\", and it was asked there: but these are words of Torah that were not given to be written? And they explained: \"Since it is not possible otherwise – 'It is time to act for the Lord, they have made void Your Torah'\". And so wrote the Mordekhai (Gittin 407), and he added that on this basis they permitted writing the passages that are said in prayer, because there are people who do not know them by heart, and they would come to neglect the Shema and prayer. And so wrote the Ra'avya (Laws of Fasts 878), the Piskei Rid (Gittin there), the Me'iri and the Agudda. And so wrote the Baḥ (283:4), and he wondered at the Shulḥan Arukh, which was stringent \"and took no account of the custom of all Israel\". And so wrote in halakha the Shakh 3 and the Taz 1.<br>After the invention of printing, some forbade writing passages, since it is easy to print a complete book of the Torah or of the Prophets (Magen Avraham, introduction to Oraḥ Ḥayim 284; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 283:11). And some say that also in print it is permitted to print passages and haftarot (Maḥatzit Ha-Shekel 284; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 284:4. And so it is implied by all the Aḥaronim who decided to permit writing passages and did not address the invention of printing). And so is the custom."},{"n":10,"en":"Some say it is forbidden to write verses for a minor purpose such as creating an educational atmosphere, since the whole permission to write verses is only for the great need of Torah study. So wrote the Taz (283:1), the Penei Yehoshua (Gittin 6b), the Birkei Yosef (283:1) and the Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 13. And so wrote the Magen Avraham (638:9), that one should not write verses for decorating the sukka. And so wrote the Baḥ (184:2), that one should not write verses in a letter of greeting.<br>On the other hand, the Shakh (283:6) brought the words of the Levush, who was lenient in writing a verse on the mantle of a Torah scroll, and wrote that the custom to be lenient has spread. And so agreed the Benei Yona (283:4), except that he wrote that it is preferable to refrain from it. And so the Bikkurei Ya'akov (638:18) permitted writing verses for decorating the sukka. And what the Shulḥan Arukh wrote (283:4): \"It is forbidden to embroider verses on a tallit\", is because the tallit is an object used for a mitzva and it is permitted to enter the lavatory with it, and if a verse is written on it – it will be treated with contempt (Shakh 283:6; see below 9:12). And the Mishna Berura (638:9) inclined not to write verses in the sukka, apparently because of the concern that they will be treated with contempt, for in siman 40:3 he permitted writing verses to hang at the entrance of the synagogue.<br>And Rav Uziel (Mishpetei Uziel 6:76) permitted writing verses for any purpose, since such is the custom, and also because two views may be combined. a) The view of the Ra'avan (Gittin 60a), that the prohibition is to write a passage, but individual verses are permitted, as is done in the Talmud. And so wrote the Korban Netanel (Megilla 2:70). (However, from the unqualified wording of the other poskim it emerges that the law of one verse is like the law of a passage, and only within the framework of writing the Oral Torah may individual verses be quoted.) b) If one writes a passage not for the sake of sanctity or study, there is no prohibition, for so wrote the Nimukei Yosef (Gittin 35a in the pages of the Rif) and the Tashbetz (1:2), based on Yevamot (106b).<br>In addition, the Tashbetz (1:2) wrote that the prohibition applies specifically to Ashurit script that is valid for a Torah scroll, but in a script that is not valid for a Torah scroll there is no prohibition on writing passages of Torah. The Pitḥei Teshuva (283:2) brought this. And so it is implied by the Mikdash Me'at 3. And although the Benei Yona (283:3) forbade it also in another script, and so is the view of several Aḥaronim, since there are those who are lenient in writing verses of the Torah for purposes that are not essential, if one writes them in a script that does not resemble the writing of the Torah scroll, one may be lenient."}]},{"slug":"21-08-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-10/","title_he":"י – שרטוט","title_en":"10. Scoring Lines (Sirtut)","body_en":"<p>Out of respect for the Torah, one must take care to write it in straight lines, and therefore it is a law given to Moshe at Sinai that it is forbidden to write it without sirtut (Gittin 6b). Sirtut is the scoring of a line on the parchment that creates rows on it, so that the scribe writes the letters with their tops touching the scored line, and in this way writes straight (below 10:5).</p>\n<p>The Sages ruled that one who writes verses in STaM letters not within the framework of writing STaM must also take care to score the first line. However, since this is not the writing of a Torah scroll or a mezuza, there is no need to score the following lines; rather, the writer straightens them on the basis of the first line, which is written straight thanks to the scoring. Likewise, since this is not writing for the mitzva of STaM, one may score the first line in pencil, and after writing the verse – one may erase the scoring. Likewise, if there was a printed line on a page, it may be considered as scoring, while taking care to write the words close against it.</p>\n<p>One who writes words of Torah that include three words of a verse in STaM script, if they are on the first line, must also take care to score before writing them.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"According to the Ramban (Gittin 6b), there is no obligation of scoring in a script that is not Ashurit (STaM script). And although the Rosh (Megilla 2:2) disputed him, the Beit Yosef (284:2) wrote that the view of many Rishonim is like the Ramban, and \"all these great authorities are worthy of being relied upon\". And so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (284:2).<br>Rabbeinu Tam wrote that only if one writes a verse on the first line must one score beneath it, but on the second line one need not score, for since the first line is already written, he can straighten the rest of the writing according to it. And so is the view of the Ramban (Gittin 6b) and the Rosh (Megilla 2:2). And although there are those who disagree (the Tashbetz 1:2), the halakha follows Rabbeinu Tam (Rema 284:2).<br>Some are stringent to require scoring even for verses used as flourishes that are written on the first line of a letter that a person sends to his friend (the Ramban, Gittin there; the Semag, positive commandments 23; and the Shakh 284:2 inclined to this). And some say that the obligation of scoring applies specifically to a verse intended for study (Tosafot, Gittin 6b, s.v. 'Amar', in the name of Rabbeinu Tam and the Riva; the Rosh; the Ran), and so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (284:2).<br>Regarding the number of words that requires scoring, it is brought in Gittin (6b): \"Rabbi Yitzḥak said: Two one may write, three one may not write. It was taught in a baraita: Three one may write, four one may not write.\" Some are lenient that only four words or more require scoring (Responsa of Rabbi Yehoshua Ha-Nagid 14; Rabbeinu Yeruḥam 2:2). But most poskim were stringent to require scoring already for three words (the Behag in Tosafot there, s.v. 'Amar Rabbi Yitzḥak'; the Rosh, Megilla 2:2; the Tashbetz 1:2; Shulḥan Arukh 284:2)."}]},{"slug":"21-08-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-08-11/","title_he":"יא – כתב אשורי (סת\"ם)","title_en":"11. Ashurit Script (STaM)","body_en":"<p>The Holy Tongue has a special quality, for it is the language of the Torah and in it God created the world. It follows that the words of the Hebrew language express the inner essence of every thing and matter in the world. The Ashurit script too, in which the Torah is written, also called 'STaM script', expresses the inner meaning of every letter and word, and secrets and allusions are hidden in it (the Rosh, Nedarim 38a; Responsa of the Radbaz 4:45).<sup>12</sup></p>\n<p>Many hold that since the Torah is written in Ashurit script, it is not fitting to use it for mundane matters (Rambam; Rema, Yoreh De'ah 284:2). Therefore people were accustomed to change mundane writing into a 'round script' (Solitreo or Mashait) or 'Rashi script' and the like, so that it looks different from the script in which the Torah is written, and then there is not the slightest disrespect in writing mundane matters in it (Rambam, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Maharam Alashkar 74).</p>\n<p>Some say that matters of mitzva too, such as ketubbot, gittin and wedding invitations, should not be written in STaM script, which is intended for words of Torah alone (Rambam, Rav Pe'alim). And some say that it is permitted to write matters of mitzva in STaM script (Ketav Sofer, Even Ha-Ezer 22). And some hold that there is no prohibition on writing mundane matters in STaM script (the Tashbetz 1:5).</p>\n<p>Our square script is not valid for STaM, and therefore it is permitted from the outset to write mundane matters in it, and it is also permitted to read mundane matters in it in the lavatory.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"There are three views in the Talmud regarding the Ashurit script (Sanhedrin 21b-22a). Some say that the Torah was given from the outset in Ashurit script, and so the tradition continued until our days. Some say that the Torah was given from the outset in Ashurit script, and mundane matters were written in the ancient Hebrew script used by the peoples of the region; but in the Babylonian exile the traditions were forgotten, and Ezra the Scribe restored the tradition of writing the Torah in Ashurit script. And some say that the Torah was given from the outset in the ancient Hebrew script, and they had a tradition that it was destined to change, and it was Ezra who changed the writing of the Torah to Ashurit script. This script is called 'Ashurit' either because it came from Ashur (Assyria), or because it is \"praised (me'ushar) in its writing\" (Sanhedrin there), or because its letters are straight (me'yusharot) (Alei Tamar, Megilla 1, based on Rabbeinu Ḥananel, Sanhedrin 22a)."},{"n":13,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Responsa, Blau edition 268) that because of the great sanctity of the Ashurit script, in which the Torah is written, \"it is disgraceful to use it except for holy writings\", and therefore \"the Sephardim changed their script, and gave the letters other forms until it became as though it were another script, so that it would be permitted to use it for mundane matters\". And so wrote Rabbeinu Yeruḥam (Toledot Adam Ve-Ḥava 2:2). Some wrote the Rambam's words in the language of a prohibition (Orḥot Ḥayim, part 1, Torah Study 9; Maharam Alashkar 74). And the Rema (Yoreh De'ah 284:2) wrote: \"And some say that one should not write mundane matters in the Ashurit script in which the Torah is written\". This is the reason people were accustomed to write mundane matters in Solitreo or Mashait script, and on the basis of this style of writing the printers created the script called 'Rashi script', in order to create a noticeable difference between the Scripture, which was printed in square letters, and the commentary; and since they printed the commentary in it, in time people began to call it Rashi script. On the other hand, the Tashbetz (1:5) wrote that it is permitted to write mundane matters in Ashurit script (and see Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 283:14; 284:8).<br>The Rambam wrote (Responsa 268), in the name of Rabbi Yosef ibn Migash, that one should not write in Ashurit script matters of mitzva that are not Torah, such as a get. And likewise in Rav Pe'alim (4, Yoreh De'ah 32) he forbade writing in Ashurit script things that might come to be treated with contempt. On the other hand, some permit writing in STaM script mitzva needs that are not Torah (Ketav Sofer, Even Ha-Ezer 22; Igrot Moshe, Yoreh De'ah 3:120). And so is the custom.<br>In our square script, since it is not valid for STaM, it is permitted to write mundane matters, for it is not the script \"in which the Torah is written\", and it is even permitted to read it in the lavatory. And so emerges from She'elat Ya'avetz (1:10) and Divrei Yosef (40:1). And so wrote the Igrot Moshe (Yoreh De'ah 3:120), the Tzitz Eliezer (15:7) and Yabi'a Omer (9, Yoreh De'ah 24). (And see Aseh Lekha Rav 5:26; Minḥat Yitzḥak 1:18:20, who were stringent not to write mundane matters in square script.)<br>The Ḥavot Ya'ir wrote (109) that one should not wipe dirt and excrement with paper bearing letters in STaM script. Thus far. And it seems that it is fitting to be stringent not to wipe excrement in the lavatory also with paper bearing Hebrew letters that are not in STaM script, since this is the Holy Tongue in which the Torah was given. But when there is no alternative, out of respect for human dignity, one may be lenient to wipe with mundane matter even if it is written in STaM script, since according to all views there is no Torah prohibition in this."}]}]});
